        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "5a81ff04-3cd8-571d-9822-a2d6e93813a7",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: \"Just the Two of Us\", \"Bette Davis Eyes\", \"In the Name of Love\", Winelight\n- People: Grover Washington Jr., Bill Withers, William Salter, Ralph MacDonald, Sheena Easton, Kim Carnes, Donna Weiss, Jackie DeShannon\n- Topics: Billboard Hot 100 chart performance\n\nFacts:\n- Jackie DeShannon recorded \"Bette Davis Eyes\" in 1974.\n- \"Just the Two of Us\" is a 1981 R&B single written by Bill Withers, William Salter, and Ralph MacDonald, and it was recorded by Grover Washington Jr. and Bill Withers.\n- Kim Carnes's 1981 version of \"Bette Davis Eyes,\" written by Donna Weiss and Jackie DeShannon, spent nine weeks at No. 1 on the Billboard Hot 100 and was Billboard's biggest hit of 1981.\n- An edited version of \"Just the Two of Us\" reached No. 2 on the Billboard Hot 100 for three weeks, behind \"Morning Train (9 to 5)\" by Sheena Easton and \"Bette Davis Eyes\" by Kim Carnes.\n- The Winelight track \"In the Name of Love\" was also released in a rearranged form without Grover Washington Jr.'s saxophone track under the name of Ralph MacDonald with Bill Withers on vocals.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4f9e830d-9b99-5f2f-89cb-52e022bbde9c",
        "text": "Page: Just the Two of Us (Grover Washington Jr. song). Sentence: \"Just the Two of Us\" is a 1981 R&B single written by Bill Withers, William Salter and Ralph MacDonald, which was recorded by Grover Washington Jr. and Bill Withers.\nPage: Just the Two of Us (Grover Washington Jr. song). Sentence: An edited version reached number two on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100, staying there for 3 weeks, behind \"Morning Train (9 to 5)\" by Sheena Easton and \"Bette Davis Eyes\" by Kim Carnes.\nPage: Bette Davis Eyes. Sentence: \"Bette Davis Eyes\" is a song written and composed by Donna Weiss and Jackie DeShannon, and made popular by American singer Kim Carnes.\nPage: Bette Davis Eyes. Sentence: DeShannon recorded it in 1974; Carnes's 1981 version spent nine weeks at No. 1 on the \"Billboard\" Hot 100 and was \"Billboard\"' s biggest hit of 1981.\nPage: Winelight. Sentence: The track \"In the Name of Love\" from the album was also released in rearranged form, without Washington's saxophone track, under the name of Ralph MacDonald and Bill Withers (on vocals).",
